#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292822 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "579373bc-38b3-584c-a118-84be7dfc9074" :knowledge-point/title "Comparing the Relative Magnitude of a Trigonometric Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5558984e-05d9-5ba1-9de2-fa71a77b8cf3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\infty " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292824} {:answer/id #uuid "460ae15b-f4be-5514-98b7-e705956a073c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292825} {:answer/id #uuid "9a6443fa-1a0b-51ee-b860-501184545d20" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{DNE}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292826} {:answer/id #uuid "2946a868-1500-5d7a-8bff-44dd42e5e7d5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292827} {:answer/id #uuid "39bbe88a-2431-597a-a713-28a16abe634b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292828}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2946a868-1500-5d7a-8bff-44dd42e5e7d5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292827} :answer-field/id #uuid "e3208868-49e5-557c-b01c-a936ff6cb30c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292823}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d9250d96-2930-55be-a93d-b3b19673faf9" :question/math-academy-id "q-106832" :question/problem "Evaluate ${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{2x-{x}^{3}}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rewrite the limit as\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{2x-{x}^{3}}={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{{x}^{3}-2x}.\n$$\n\nThe numerator here is a bounded, oscillating function with\n\n$$\n|\\operatorname{sin}⁡x|\\le 1.\n$$\n\nHowever, the denominator ${x}^{3}-2x$ grows without bound as $x\\to \\infty .$ So the denominator is growing much faster than the numerator, and we conclude that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{2x-{x}^{3}}={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{{x}^{3}-2x}=0.\n$$\n\nWe can see this from the graph of $y=\\frac{-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{2x-{x}^{3}},$ shown below:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14029498/assets/a9d96a5a85181cddc6eb1bd5f79d6a0238ef16f060ddebf2dc4c48ef1911604f.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292829 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0a166115-1a93-5945-bfac-b1d3ddb1cfbd" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Cross Product of Two Vectors Whose Angle Is a Special Angle"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "653644b0-5342-5dd7-ba29-a7cfaeade2ce" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12n" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292831} {:answer/id #uuid "c1cb6b65-0f97-5d5f-8b22-b8890e6797d7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4n" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292832} {:answer/id #uuid "edaaac1a-88e2-518e-833f-77dc5e09265d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "18n" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292833} {:answer/id #uuid "137c510c-446c-5ac4-9206-222c6493612a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6n" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292834} {:answer/id #uuid "a9f8ed7d-5bf0-5dcc-b9b7-d27afae13f7a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "24n" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292835}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "653644b0-5342-5dd7-ba29-a7cfaeade2ce" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12n" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292831} :answer-field/id #uuid "5ba727a4-81fb-5a56-abce-ea644f58432e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292830}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "6031cc66-ec95-5415-9785-9f9e09ec2735" :question/math-academy-id "q-127838" :question/problem "The vector $u$ has magnitude $6$, the vector $v$ has magnitude $4$, the angle between $u$ and $v$ is ${30}^{∘}$ and $n$ is the unit vector that points in the direction of $u\\times v.$ Find $u\\times v$ in terms of $n.$" :question/worked-solution "We use the formula for the cross product and obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\times v & =|u|\\cdot |v|\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\cdot n \\\\  & =6\\cdot 4\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡{30}^{∘}\\cdot n \\\\  & =24\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\cdot n \\\\  & =12n.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292836 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "63426ad7-4275-5ac3-854d-e91ec00dcbe3" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Equation of a Trendline"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "de8710da-d1c1-520a-ac96-3582c83f6d7a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292838} {:answer/id #uuid "994e93e7-c00a-5742-8a68-75269590ca63" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292839} {:answer/id #uuid "d43841e2-859c-5834-b8b9-9fc0694c41b7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292840} {:answer/id #uuid "95660e11-a29a-5099-8d9d-0dc2000b2ab4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292841} {:answer/id #uuid "5158c879-7858-5855-87a8-1fd6c3fabb37" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292842}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d43841e2-859c-5834-b8b9-9fc0694c41b7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292840} :answer-field/id #uuid "de8facb1-7b54-5d95-8b90-671f98bba8ce" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292837}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0a2a1e0f-109b-5eb1-aede-0460221885ac" :question/math-academy-id "q-134016" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14029498/assets/e2d51071ddc198adecdff56593dec29ba9aef10d90b89ddc03a4cdbe9c757034.png)\n\nWhat is the slope of the trend line shown above?" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the trend line passes through the points $({x}_{1},{y}_{1})=(0,8)$ and $({x}_{2},{y}_{2})=(3,7).$\n\nTherefore, we compute the slope $m$ of the line, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & =\\frac{{y}_{2}-{y}_{1}}{{x}_{2}-{x}_{1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{7-8}{3-0} \\\\  & =\\frac{-1}{3} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292843 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "87f80908-037b-56ae-a2b5-59bdc49f0acb" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Cartesian Coordinates of a Point Given a Description of the Radius and Angle"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0019a12c-e576-5a4b-b94b-3337d9bd793e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292845} {:answer/id #uuid "b25b450c-fcf5-5490-96c8-5403facd11e4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3},-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292846} {:answer/id #uuid "b0cbd1d3-638c-56ed-96b1-0b3edcc865f0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{6},-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{6})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292847} {:answer/id #uuid "0d70bf82-a79c-5359-bc83-3c6414396490" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{5},-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{5})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292848} {:answer/id #uuid "9b29f011-514a-5612-b515-2c1d43c38312" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4},-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292849}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b0cbd1d3-638c-56ed-96b1-0b3edcc865f0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{6},-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{6})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292847} :answer-field/id #uuid "b636fb6d-c682-542b-b25c-749bd6dcf1f0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292844}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1600c079-8696-5fa4-9f4f-536830552848" :question/math-academy-id "q-17124" :question/problem "Given that the distance from a point $P$ to the origin is $\\frac{1}{3},$ and that $P$ makes an angle of $\\frac{5\\pi }{4}$ with the positive $x$-axis, what are the Cartesian coordinates of $P?$" :question/worked-solution "We notice that the point $P$ is described using polar coordinates $(r,\\theta ),$ where\n\n$$\nr=\\frac{1}{3},\\,\\theta =\\frac{5\\pi }{4}.\n$$\n\nTo convert from polar coordinates to Cartesian coordinates, we use\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the $x$-coordinate of $P:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ x & =\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{4}) \\\\ x & =\\frac{1}{3}(\\frac{-\\sqrt{2}}{2}) \\\\ x & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen we calculate the $y$-coordinate of $P:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ y & =\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{4}) \\\\ y & =\\frac{1}{3}(\\frac{-\\sqrt{2}}{2}) \\\\ y & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates of $P$ are $(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{6},-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{6}).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292850 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5cf64df7-0abb-5b42-bed6-b3664c2de89a" :knowledge-point/title "Matching a Single Coefficient"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4b720bf6-ab1f-5f5b-b28b-bd2d9226c53b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292852} {:answer/id #uuid "838eda95-a363-598c-b42f-c95bc7f93707" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292853} {:answer/id #uuid "d8b50d2d-73a8-50e5-be21-8640d6f0cf0a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292854} {:answer/id #uuid "0db79e22-5124-5430-8d80-42a1d499b503" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292855} {:answer/id #uuid "95698a32-4eb8-5163-b62c-1cbbf4c09e8f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292856}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "95698a32-4eb8-5163-b62c-1cbbf4c09e8f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292856} :answer-field/id #uuid "0931fc79-d008-5b67-8515-1a375ae7f283" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292851}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f0b2734f-0ee6-5285-8bef-0e313bb618ab" :question/math-academy-id "q-288444" :question/problem "The following equation is true for all values of $x.$\n\n$$\n-2{x}^{2}+3x+b=-2{x}^{2}+3x-6\n$$\n\nWhat is the value of the constant $b?$" :question/worked-solution "Since the equation is true for all values of $x,$ the expressions on both sides must be exactly the same. That means the coefficients of the matching powers of $x$ must be equal.\n\nWe compare the constant terms on both sides. On the left-hand side, the constant is $b.$ On the right-hand side, the constant is $-6.$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\nb=-6.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292857 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "223e29c9-1bb5-51ee-9ec8-4d5c15363951" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Center of a Horizontal Hyperbola Given an Equation in Expanded Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b8e3be0f-25a5-51fe-a9ad-5cbaebe7ef10" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292859} {:answer/id #uuid "f1af8e26-b3f1-5767-9bbb-95792354f892" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292860}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b8e3be0f-25a5-51fe-a9ad-5cbaebe7ef10" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292859} :answer-field/id #uuid "ed6a1d8b-b783-5448-957c-feea21ab1621" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292858} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "24c5fd38-fad6-58fe-9efa-6cb6358a5d06" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292862}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "24c5fd38-fad6-58fe-9efa-6cb6358a5d06" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292862} :answer-field/id #uuid "4c460dcd-9be9-542b-8990-d412f56cf644" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292861}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "dd806166-c3df-538e-871b-d9477c5bfc0b" :question/math-academy-id "q-328382" :question/problem "The center of the hyperbola ${x}^{2}-9{y}^{2}-6x-36y=54$ is$\\,\\,($\n{{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}\n$).$" :question/worked-solution "The equation of a horizontal hyperbola centered at $(h,k)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{(x-h)^{2}}{{a}^{2}}-\\frac{(y-k)^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\nTo rewrite the equation of the hyperbola in standard form, we need to group $x$ and $y$ terms and complete the squares as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-9{y}^{2}-6x-36y & =54 \\\\ {x}^{2}-6x-9{y}^{2}-36y & =54 \\\\ [{x}^{2}-6x]-9[{y}^{2}+4y] & =54 \\\\ [(x-3)^{2}-9]-9[(y+2)^{2}-4] & =54 \\\\ (x-3)^{2}-9-9(y+2)^{2}+36 & =54 \\\\ (x-3)^{2}-9(y+2)^{2} & =54+9-36 \\\\ (x-3)^{2}-9(y+2)^{2} & =27 \\\\ \\frac{(x-3)^{2}}{(\\sqrt{27})^{2}}-\\frac{(y+2)^{2}}{(\\sqrt{3})^{2}} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x-3)^{2}}{(\\sqrt{27})^{2}}-\\frac{(y-(-2))^{2}}{(\\sqrt{3})^{2}} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the center of the hyperbola is $(\\,3,-2\\,).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292863 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5c6490a5-4923-58e8-99c3-d568a033800e" :knowledge-point/title "Computing Coefficients in a Partial Fractions Decomposition"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b84caf6b-47a6-51dd-bc22-d93398b42e45" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292865}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b84caf6b-47a6-51dd-bc22-d93398b42e45" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292865} :answer-field/id #uuid "c7354aa1-1525-55a5-8a28-58ba28267f4f" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292864}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "55bbc0e5-8d38-5973-b1f1-afc64d12e238" :question/math-academy-id "q-351278" :question/problem "Express $\\frac{9{x}^{2}-6x+20}{(2{x}^{2}+5)x}$ as a sum of partial fractions.\n\n| $\\frac{9{x}^{2}-6x+20}{(2{x}^{2}+5)x}=\\,$ | $x\\,-\\,${{field-1}} | $+$ | $4$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | $2{x}^{2}+5$ |  | $x$ |" :question/worked-solution "Our task is to find $B$ such that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{9{x}^{2}-6x+20}{(2{x}^{2}+5)x}=\\frac{x+B}{2{x}^{2}+5}+\\frac{4}{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides by $(2{x}^{2}+5)x$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9{x}^{2}-6x+20 & =(x+B)x+4(2{x}^{2}+5).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor $x=1$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9(1)^{2}-6(1)+20 & =(1+B)(1)+4(2(1)^{2}+5) \\\\ 23 & =1+B+28 \\\\ B & =-6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n\\frac{9{x}^{2}-6x+20}{(2{x}^{2}+5)x}=\\frac{x-6}{2{x}^{2}+5}+\\frac{4}{x}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292866 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "240a0a0b-fb86-5760-bb2b-74275708ff77" :knowledge-point/title "Simplifying an Expression Using the Alternate Forms of the Cotangent-Cosecant Identity"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2c38ed34-fd0e-5248-b6cf-1bdf0286e986" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{csc}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292868} {:answer/id #uuid "643a58f1-4de4-5b5d-96e1-23780f14359e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{csc}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292869} {:answer/id #uuid "6e521150-7bb5-5ca1-a819-2d10fb4f9843" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1+\\operatorname{csc}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292870} {:answer/id #uuid "a23c4fb3-8b28-5e2b-af17-42a557a791a4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1-\\operatorname{csc}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292871} {:answer/id #uuid "de4bfde3-a56f-5f58-842d-0df431407c64" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{csc}^{2}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292872}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2c38ed34-fd0e-5248-b6cf-1bdf0286e986" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{csc}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292868} :answer-field/id #uuid "3a051f82-005b-5382-8325-838241e21514" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292867}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "e148983a-5035-5901-aa4f-54a3e738d0a1" :question/math-academy-id "q-65463" :question/problem "Simplify $\\frac{{cot}^{2}⁡x}{\\operatorname{csc}⁡x-1}-1.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we multiply the numerator and the denominator of the fraction by $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x+1.$ This gives: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{cot}^{2}⁡x}{\\operatorname{csc}⁡x-1} & =\\frac{{cot}^{2}⁡x}{\\operatorname{csc}⁡x-1}\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{csc}⁡x+1}{\\operatorname{csc}⁡x+1} \\\\  & =\\frac{{cot}^{2}⁡x\\,(\\operatorname{csc}⁡x+1)}{{csc}^{2}⁡x-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the identity ${csc}^{2}⁡\\theta -1={cot}^{2}⁡\\theta $, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{cot}^{2}⁡x\\,(\\operatorname{csc}⁡x+1)}{{csc}^{2}⁡x-1}-1 & =\\frac{{cot}^{2}⁡x\\,(\\operatorname{csc}⁡x+1)}{{cot}^{2}⁡x}-1 \\\\  & =\\frac{\\cancel{{cot}^{2}⁡x}\\,(\\operatorname{csc}⁡x+1)}{\\cancel{{cot}^{2}⁡x}}-1 \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{csc}⁡x+1)\\,-1 \\\\  & =\\operatorname{csc}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292873 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "730fc9a7-a1b8-55a4-b8d3-666f87b82958" :knowledge-point/title "Dividing a Complex Number by an Imaginary Number"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0f928061-3e27-50b8-b05b-3f31993e7d07" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{4}-\\frac{1}{2}\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292875} {:answer/id #uuid "47646cbc-679f-5db7-bf84-f66a87e574cf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}-\\frac{3}{2}\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292876} {:answer/id #uuid "47479dd7-0df2-55b4-894e-65a9f3609b39" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}-\\frac{1}{4}\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292877} {:answer/id #uuid "29cd0a60-5cd2-5e5f-9dfa-e21c15775b50" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}+\\frac{3}{2}\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292878} {:answer/id #uuid "e0c9cbfe-e143-5378-9dd3-08fb363427d2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}-\\frac{3}{2}\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292879}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "47646cbc-679f-5db7-bf84-f66a87e574cf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}-\\frac{3}{2}\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292876} :answer-field/id #uuid "33d41a29-1d36-570c-906a-cdc8820aada9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292874}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b360ffce-06f6-5c3e-8fff-4c161220f31e" :question/math-academy-id "q-74654" :question/problem "$\\frac{6+\\text{i}}{4\\text{i}}=$" :question/worked-solution "Here, the denominator is the imaginary number $4\\text{i}.$ Note that this can be written as $0+4\\text{i},$ so the conjugate is $0-4\\text{i},$ or simply $-4\\text{i}.$\n\nSo, we multiply both the numerator and the denominator by $-4\\text{i},$ so that the denominator turns into a real number:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{6+\\text{i}}{4\\text{i}} & =\\frac{6+\\text{i}}{4\\text{i}}\\cdot \\frac{-4\\text{i}}{-4\\text{i}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-24\\text{i}-4{\\text{i}}^{2}}{-16{\\text{i}}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-24\\text{i}-4(-1)}{-16(-1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{4-24\\text{i}}{16}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that the denominator is a real number, we can write the complex number in the form $a+b\\text{i}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4-24\\text{i}}{16} & =\\frac{4}{16}-\\frac{24}{16}\\text{i} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}-\\frac{3}{2}\\text{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
